    "message": {
      "content": "# Validation Checklist for Multi-Node Language-Model Serving Systems\n\n## Purpose and Scope\n\nThis checklist validates that a distributed LLM serving system (tensor-parallel, pipeline-parallel, expert-parallel, or data-parallel deployments) behaves correctly under load, fails predictably, and produces trustworthy performance measurements. It assumes a cluster of N nodes, each running one or more serving processes, coordinated by a scheduler or orchestrator (e.g., Kubernetes, Slurm, Ray, or a custom launcher).\n\n---\n\n## 1. Configuration Identity\n\n**Goal:** Prove that every node is running the *same* logical system before attributing any behavior to the distributed architecture.\n\n- [ ] **Model artifact hash.** Record SHA-256 of model weights, tokenizer files, and config files on every node. All hashes must match. Verify *after* any download/copy step, not just at build time.\n- [ ] **Code and container identity.** Record container image digests (not tags), git commit SHAs, and build timestamps for all binaries. Tags are mutable; digests are not.\n- [ ] **Runtime version parity.** Framework versions (e.g., inference engine, CUDA/ROCm, NCCL/Gloo, driver versions, Python version) must be identical across nodes. Log them at startup and diff.\n- [ ] **Parallelism configuration.** Record tensor-parallel size (TP), pipeline-parallel size (PP), expert-parallel size (EP), data-parallel size (DP), and their product. Confirm the product equals the number of model-replica shards you expect, and that the launcher's world size matches.\n- [ ] **Deterministic settings documented.** Note whether sampling temperature, seeds, and any nondeterministic kernels (e.g., certain fused attention or reduction paths) are enabled. This affects reproducibility claims later.\n- [ ] **Quantization/precision flags.** If any node applies different precision (fp16",
